# Documentation Voice

Write docs in plain, direct English. Sound like an engineer explaining the
system to a peer — not like marketing copy or a chatbot summary.

Chinese (`zh-Hans`) pages should read the same way in 简体中文: short
sentences, concrete terms, no translationese. See `.cursor/rules/docs-i18n.mdc`
for locale file layout.

- Prefer short sentences and concrete nouns. Cut filler ("In today's world",
  "It's important to note", "This powerful platform").
- State the problem, then the mechanism. Do not stack synonyms or repeat the
  same idea in three forms.
- Use Dex terms exactly: Flow, Step, Attribute, Channel, RPC, Timer.
- Write API names, method names, types, and identifiers in inline code in
  prose (for example `WaitFor`, `StepDecision`, `InvokeRPC`). Use **bold** for a
  term's first definition and for UI labels.
- Diagrams and examples should carry structure; prose should not over-explain
  what the diagram already shows.
- Avoid bullet lists when a paragraph reads better. Avoid numbered lists unless
  order matters.

## Concept pages and versions

Each fact has one home. See Docs Information Placement in
`.cursor/rules/project-core.mdc`.

- Concept pages are the Concepts, Components, and Design pattern pages. Use
  short sentences there. Put one idea in each sentence.
- Concept pages contain no version numbers and no compatibility notes for
  older servers or SDKs.
- Say each thing once. Link to its single home instead of repeating it.
- Version differences appear only in the Developer reference. Mark each one
  "Since <component version>" or "Changed in <component version>". Use the
  version of the component the page documents, such as "Since 1.35" for an
  SDK, cli, or web, and "Since 0.1.17" for server-zd.

## Read pages for humans before merge

- Every changed page outside the Developer reference must be read in a
  browser before merge. This includes Intro, Quick start, Concepts,
  Components, Design patterns, and Use cases. Run `cd docs && npm start`,
  which builds and serves both locales. Read every such page from top to
  bottom as a reader, once in English and once in Simplified Chinese. Fix what
  you find. List the pages you read in the pull request's Testing section.
- Every docs change must work on a phone as well as a desktop. This covers
  content, components, styles, diagrams, and the sidebar. Check each changed
  page, or each page type a style or component change affects, at a 390px wide
  viewport. The page must not scroll sideways. Text stays readable without
  zooming. Wide tables and code scroll inside their own box. Diagrams fit or
  scroll inside their own box. The docs menu opens and reaches every page.
  List the phone checks in the pull request's Testing section.

## Page structure and voice

These rules come from the docs revisit plan,
`docs/design/plan/docs-revisit.md`. The plan has the reasons and examples.

### Reading speeds

- The opening has at most three sentences. The first says what the thing is
  or what the reader has at the end. The second says when to use it or why it
  matters. The first sentence must read as complete on its own, because it
  becomes the page description, the `llms.txt` entry, and the search snippet.
- The headings alone make an outline. Task sections use an imperative verb,
  such as "Skip a Timer". Concept sections use a noun, such as "Guarantees".
  Do not use "Overview", "Introduction", "Details", or "More" as a section
  heading. Keep headings to six words or fewer.
- The common case comes first. Order sections as what it is, using it, edge
  cases, and limits. Failure matrices, option lists, defaults, limits, and
  version notes go to the Developer reference. The page keeps one sentence and
  a link.
- At a 390px width, the first screen says what the page is about and where to
  go next.
- Headings are statements, not questions. Write "Start here" or "Common
  tasks", not "First time here?" or "What do you want to do?". A section on
  why to use something lists what it provides, feature by feature, not the
  scenarios a reader might be in.
- A group overview page is a wayfinder. It says what the group covers and
  links to each child page and its key sections. It does not repeat what a
  child page owns: per-item caveats, limits, API and option names, numbers,
  steps, or term definitions. A table on an overview has only columns that
  help a reader choose and go, such as name, language, and link. Terms go in
  the Glossary, and the overview links to them.
- A page's opening and the first paragraph of each section get the reader
  going on that section's job. Keep only essential links there, such as a
  prerequisite to follow first. Move "see X for the full code" pointers,
  caveats, and version notes to the end of the section or to Next.
- Charts carry names and short labels, not sentences. Put the explanation in
  the text around the chart.
- Chart colors have fixed roles: green is Dex, blue is a third-party service
  or framework, grey is your own code, and amber marks a failure or crash.

### Page skeletons

| Page type | Sections, in order |
| --- | --- |
| Concept | Opening, When to use it, Guarantees, The smallest example, Common tasks, Reference links |
| Design pattern | Use it when (one sentence and one scenario), Flow graph, How it works, Code, Rules and pitfalls, Related |
| Product example | A "Shows:" line, Flow graph, Product requirements in product terms only, Flow design, Implementation, Run it |
| Framework page | What the adapter does, Install, How a turn maps to Steps, How this framework differs, Limits |
| How-to and operate | Goal, Before you start, Numbered steps with expected output, Check it worked, Troubleshooting |
| Comparison | What they share, then for each tool: term map, the same example, differences, when the other fits |

### Voice

| Sentence type | Form | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Definition | The thing is the subject. | A Timer is a durable wait until a point in time. |
| Instruction | An imperative. "Your" marks the reader's code. | To run a waiting Step early, skip its Timer. Call **SkipTimer** from your Client. |
| Behavior | Dex or a component is the subject. | Dex treats a skipped Timer as fired. |
| Condition or outcome | "You" is allowed. | If the Worker stops, you see the Step as Waiting in Dex Web. |

Use the present tense and the active voice. Do not write "can" for something
that always happens. Do not phrase an instruction as a fact, such as "A Client
can skip a Timer".

### Sentences

- One clause per sentence. Show cause and effect by order: the cause first,
  the effect in the next sentence. A sentence may start with "So" or "Then".
  A short sentence that stands alone carries emphasis.
- Put the condition first: "If the ship retries run out, the Flow moves to
  RefundStep."
- Name the Step, the number, and the default. Say what to do before what not
  to do.
- Each paragraph, list item, and callout opens with what is true. A negation
  comes second, as a clarification. Cut a negation that only restates what the
  positive already implies, such as "dexcli does not run on Windows" after a
  macOS or Linux prerequisite. A real warning whose point is the negation keeps it:
  list its opening words under `positive_first_allow:` in the page's front
  matter, with a YAML comment that says why.
  `npm run lint:structure` (tests/docs-positive-first.mjs) enforces this.
- Define a term on first use, or link to it. The Glossary is the home of every
  term.
- Requirement lists use product terms only. The Flow design section maps each
  requirement to Dex.
- Write "Flow run", not "Flow execution". Write "Dex Server" on first use,
  then "the server". Do not write "simply", "just", "easily", "seamless",
  "powerful", "robust", "leverage", or "note that".

### Structure

- Docs pages never link to a README in the repository; link an example's folder.
  Clicking a sidebar group header opens that group's overview page, which is not
  listed again under it and is titled with the group's name; no item is labelled
  "Overview". Every page in the reading sequence (not a group overview, the home
  page, or the Glossary) ends with one section named Next (下一步), and
  following its first link gives a linear reading order. The home page links
  straight to the one page that does each job, with no ordered reading lists.
  The Quick Start group's header opens the Quick Start tutorial, which owns the
  install; it is the one exception to the wayfinder rule. `npm run lint:structure` (tests/docs-structure.mjs) enforces this.

### Formatting, code, and diagrams

- Identifiers use inline code (D8). Bold is kept only for a term's first
  definition and for UI labels.
- Use a table when three or more items share two or more attributes, with at
  most four columns. Use a list for parallel items, numbered only when order
  matters. Use a paragraph for reasoning.
- Use a `warning` callout only for data loss, a double effect, or a security
  risk. Never put a required step in a `tip`. Use at most one callout per
  screen.
- Put one lead-in sentence before each snippet that says what to notice. Show
  the smallest excerpt and link the full file. Show "What changes in your code"
  as a diff. Put a command's expected output in a separate `text` block.
- Draw a diagram for a relationship or a sequence in time. The prose says what
  to look at, not what the diagram shows.
- No fact exists only in an image, a collapsed section, or a tab that the
  Markdown export drops. Page text never gives instructions to coding agents.
  A copyable prompt block has four parts: the goal, the source URL, the steps,
  and the rules.

### Rewriting a page

1. Classify the page by its page type. Split a page that holds two types.
2. List every fact before writing: behaviors, rules, guarantees, defaults,
   errors, and limits.
3. Place each fact. It stays, moves to the reference, or merges into its one
   home. A dropped fact needs a reason in the pull request.
4. Write the opening and the headings first, then the body.
5. Check each sentence against the 2026-11 two questions and the rules above.
6. Compare the result with the fact list. List technical doubts separately.
   Never change them quietly.
7. Check the Markdown export, the 390px width, and the Chinese page.
8. In the pull request, list the moved anchors, the redirects, and the doubts.
